# One line-ending convention for the whole repository.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# The repo had drifted to a near-even split - 97 files LF against 71 CRLF, with
# .js at an exact 40/40 tie. Any editor or script that rewrote a file in the
# other style then produced a whole-file diff: a two-line favicon change arrived
# as ~9,700 changed lines. That buries real edits, makes review impossible, and
# has already caused repeated confusion in this project.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores LF in the repository for everything Git detects as
# text, and checks out LF regardless of each person's core.autocrlf setting. A
# tool that flips a file to CRLF locally now produces NO diff at all instead of
# rewriting the file. Nothing here needs CRLF - there are no .bat/.cmd/.ps1
# files, and browsers, Python and Node are all indifferent to it.
#
# Applied once with: git add --renormalize .

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched. text=auto detects these correctly on its own,
# but being explicit means a stray byte pattern can never get a PNG line-ending
# "normalized" into corruption.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf  binary
